This peer constructs explicit projection pairs for binary products and sums, reducing them to function spaces and to products involving the three-element truth domain.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`funToPair`).** A function from the truth domain to \(E\) determines a pair of elements of \(E\) by evaluation at truth and falsity.

*Proof.* Pair the two evaluation maps obtained by adjoining the constant true and false arguments. ∎

**Definition (`sumToProdT`).** An element of the separated sum \(E+E\) determines a triple consisting of its truth-valued tag and its two possible component projections.

*Proof.* Pair the tag map with the pair of the left and right output maps. ∎

**Definition (`swapProdT`).** The product \((E\times E)\times T\) maps to \(T\times(E\times E)\) by exchanging its two factors.

*Proof.* Pair the second projection with the first projection. ∎

**Definition (`pairToFun`).** A pair \((x,y)\) determines the truth-indexed function that returns \(x\) at truth, \(y\) at falsity, and the least element at the indeterminate truth value.

*Proof.* Curry the conditional map after exchanging the truth value and the pair. ∎

**Theorem (`TD_bot_eq_botElt`).** The least element of the truth domain is its indeterminate truth element.

*Proof.* Each is below the other: one by leastness and the other by the defining least-element property of the indeterminate truth value. ∎

**Theorem (`TD_trichotomy`).** Every element of the truth domain is either indeterminate, true, or false.

*Proof.* This is the element classification for the neighbourhood system generated by two tokens. ∎

**Theorem (`funSpace_dom_trianglelefteq`).** If \(A\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(B\), then \([A\to C]\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \([B\to C]\).

*Proof.* A projection pair from \(A\) to \(B\) induces, by contravariance in the argument, a projection pair between the two function spaces; the exponential composition and monotonicity laws verify its equations. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₀_comp_inMap₁`).** Projecting the left component after injecting a right-hand element into a separated sum gives the constant least element of the left domain.

*Proof.* A right injection has no left information beyond the master neighbourhood, so its left output is exactly the least element. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₁_comp_inMap₀`).** Projecting the right component after injecting a left-hand element into a separated sum gives the constant least element of the right domain.

*Proof.* A left injection has no right information beyond the master neighbourhood, so its right output is exactly the least element. ∎

**Theorem (`pair_bot`).** Pairing the least elements of two neighbourhood domains gives the least element of their product.

*Proof.* Every neighbourhood in the pair is forced to be the product master neighbourhood, while leastness supplies the converse order. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_left_trianglelefteq`).** If \(A\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(B\), then \(A\times C\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(B\times C\).

*Proof.* Apply the projection pair for \(A\) and \(B\) to the first coordinate and leave the second coordinate fixed; the product order verifies the retraction and approximation laws coordinatewise. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_right_trianglelefteq`).** If \(A\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(B\), then \(C\times A\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(C\times B\).

*Proof.* Commute both products, use preservation in the left coordinate, and compose the resulting subsystem embeddings. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_bot_le`).** If an approximable map relates the source master neighbourhood only to the target master neighbourhood, then the image of the source least element lies below the target least element.

*Proof.* Membership in the image of the least element reduces to a relation from the source master neighbourhood, which by hypothesis forces the target master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₀_bot_le`).** The left output of the least element of a separated sum lies below the least element of the left summand.

*Proof.* The left part of the sum master neighbourhood is the left master neighbourhood, so the preceding least-image criterion applies. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₁_bot_le`).** The right output of the least element of a separated sum lies below the least element of the right summand.

*Proof.* The right part of the sum master neighbourhood is the right master neighbourhood, so the least-image criterion applies. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_funToPair`).** On elements, the map from truth-indexed functions to pairs sends \(\varphi\) to \((\varphi(\mathsf{true}),\varphi(\mathsf{false}))\).

*Proof.* Evaluate the two components of the defining paired map. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_sumToProdT`).** On elements, the sum encoding sends \(w\) to the pair formed by its tag and the pair of its left and right outputs.

*Proof.* This follows directly from the element action of paired maps. ∎

**Theorem (`condSum_comp_sumToProdT`).** Decoding the tag-and-components representation of a separated-sum element returns that element.

*Proof.* The conditional reconstruction theorem selects the component designated by the tag of the original sum element. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_swapProdT`).** The factor-exchange map sends \(((x,y),t)\) to \((t,(x,y))\).

*Proof.* Compute the two product projections of the input pair. ∎

**Theorem (`toApproxMap_pairToFun`).** Applying the truth-indexed function represented by \((x,y)\) to \(t\) is the conditional value determined by \((t,(x,y))\).

*Proof.* Uncurrying the definition reduces evaluation to the conditional map after factor exchange. ∎

**Theorem (`funToPair_comp_pairToFun`).** Encoding a pair as a truth-indexed function and then evaluating it at truth and falsity returns the original pair.

*Proof.* The conditional laws at truth and falsity recover the first and second coordinates respectively. ∎

**Theorem (`pairToFun_comp_funToPair_le`).** Reconstructing a truth-indexed function from its values at truth and falsity gives a function below the original one.

*Proof.* By trichotomy of truth values, the reconstructed function agrees at truth and falsity, while at the indeterminate value it yields the least element. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_trianglelefteq_funSpace_TD`).** The product \(E\times E\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of the function space \([T\to E]\).

*Proof.* The pair-to-function and function-to-pair maps satisfy the retraction equation and the required approximation inequality. ∎

**Theorem (`prod_trianglelefteq_of`).** If the truth domain embeds as an isomorphic subsystem of \(E\), and \([E\to E]\) does likewise, then \(E\times E\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(E\).

*Proof.* Embed the product into \([T\to E]\), enlarge the function-space argument from \(T\) to \(E\), and then use the assumed function-space embedding. ∎

**Theorem (`which_bot_le`).** The tag of the least separated-sum element lies below the indeterminate truth value.

*Proof.* The tag map sends the sum master neighbourhood only to the truth master neighbourhood, so the least-image criterion applies. ∎

**Theorem (`which_inMap₀_le_trueElt`).** The tag of an injected left-hand element lies below truth.

*Proof.* Any truth neighbourhood appearing in that tag is either the master or the true token; a false token would force an impossible inclusion of a left injection into a right injection. ∎

**Theorem (`which_inMap₁_le_falseElt`).** The tag of an injected right-hand element lies below falsity.

*Proof.* Any truth neighbourhood appearing in that tag is either the master or the false token; a true token would force an impossible inclusion of a right injection into a left injection. ∎

**Theorem (`sumToProdT_comp_condSum_le`).** Encoding after conditionally decoding a tag and two candidate components gives an approximation below the original triple.

*Proof.* Split on the three truth values. At the indeterminate value all outputs are least; at truth the left component is recovered and the right is least; at falsity the right component is recovered and the left is least. ∎

**Theorem (`sum_trianglelefteq_prodT`).** The separated sum \(E+E\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(T\times(E\times E)\).

*Proof.* The sum encoding and conditional decoding form a projection pair by the decoding identity and encoding approximation. ∎

**Theorem (`sum_trianglelefteq_of`).** If the truth domain and the self-function-space of \(E\) are each isomorphic to subsystems of \(E\), then the separated sum \(E+E\) is isomorphic to a subsystem of \(E\).

*Proof.* First embed the sum into \(T\times(E\times E)\), replace \(T\) and \(E\times E\) by their embeddings into \(E\), and compose the resulting product embeddings. ∎
